.TH FRAMETEST 1
.SH NAME
frametest \- check frame(2) against the closed forms
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B frametest
[
.B -v
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Frametest
checks
.IR frame (2)
and prints
.B OK
if everything agrees.
.B -v
prints each measured number.
.PP
A cantilever with a load on its end deflects
.IR PL \u\s-23\s+2\d/3\fIEI\fP
and turns through
.IR PL \u\s-22\s+2\d/2\fIEI\fP.
Simply supported with a load in the middle it is
.IR PL \u\s-23\s+2\d/48\fIEI\fP,
and under a uniform load
5\fIwL\fP\u\s-24\s+2\d/384\fIEI\fP.
A bar stretches
.IR PL / EA .
These are the first results in mechanics of materials, and they are
exact rather than approximate: a Hermite beam element reproduces the
cubic exactly, so the answer
.I at the nodes
is the textbook number to rounding however few elements are used.
That is checked directly \- one element and two give tip deflections
that agree to twelve figures \- and it means a disagreement here is a
bug and never discretisation.
.PP
The truss is the textbook triangle, 8 across and 3 up, pinned at one end
and on a roller at the other with a load hung from the apex.
Its member forces are written in the test as the calculation a student
does by resolving at a joint, not as numbers copied from a run: the
bottom chord comes to +666.67 N and the diagonals to \-833.33 N.
.SS Two checks that no formula gives
A statically determinate truss's member forces follow from equilibrium
alone and do not depend on
.I E
or
.I A
at all.
Multiplying both by large factors must leave every force unchanged,
while the displacements shrink in exact proportion.
.PP
That check would prove nothing if the forces never depended on the
sections, so it is paired with an
.I indeterminate
truss \- the same triangle with a second one below it \- where changing
one member's area by nine times
.I must
change the forces, and the test fails if it does not.
.PP
And every case is checked for equilibrium: the loads and reactions must
sum to zero in both directions and in moment about the origin, which is
not a special point of any of these structures.
.SS The refusal that nothing else reaches
A bar along
.I x
pushed along
.I x
has a far end with no vertical stiffness and no vertical load.
The matrix is not singular, so nothing catches that except the rule that
a translation with nothing resisting it is a mechanism.
Every other refusal here \- no supports, two rollers \- is caught by the
factorisation first, so this is the only case that reaches the rule, and
without it the rule can be deleted with every test still passing.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/frametest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR frame (2),
.IR linalg (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Each disagreement prints a line beginning
.B FAIL
naming what was measured, what came out and what was wanted; the count
follows and the program raises an exception.
.SH BUGS
Every structure here is small \- three nodes or four.
Nothing checks that the assembly is right for a structure large enough
for a numbering mistake to show, and nothing measures conditioning.
.PP
The distributed load is checked only on a horizontal member, where the
member's own direction and the global one coincide.
A
.B udl
on an inclined member exercises the transformation of the consistent
load vector, and is not tested.
